<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Work &amp; Community Engagement Requirements &mdash; the New Law</h2>
    <p class="lede">Created by section 71119 of the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (P.L. 119-21, July 4, 2025), codified at section 1902(xx) of the Social Security Act, and implemented by CMS interim final rule CMS-2454-IFC (June 1, 2026; published June 3, 2026 at 91 FR 33348; effective July 31, 2026).</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="lawtable-h">
    <h3 id="lawtable-h">What the law and rule require</h3>
    {{TABLE:work-requirements-law.csv::Federal law and CMS rule provisions}}
    <div class="panel accent">
      <h3>The rule is hours-or-income &mdash; not earned income</h3>
      <p>Nothing in the law requires earned income. A <em>qualifying month</em> is satisfied by 80 hours of qualifying activity (including <strong>structured unpaid community service</strong>), by <strong>at least half-time school enrollment</strong>, by any combination totaling 80 hours, or by <strong>MAGI-based monthly income of at least $580</strong> (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-435/section-435.552" target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 CFR 435.552(f)</a>) &mdash; which DHCS's member outreach letter describes as income <strong>&ldquo;from any source.&rdquo;</strong> Net rental income, savings interest, and net Kalshi trading gains therefore satisfy the income standard without any wages. In California you need that showing for <strong>any one month</strong> per renewal period (new applicants: the month immediately before applying) &mdash; not every month. <em>See <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a> for the remaining open items: month-by-month trading documentation, six-month averaging scope, and trading / own-property-management hours.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="exempt-h">
    <h3 id="exempt-h">Exemptions and deemed-compliant months</h3>
    <p>If an exemption applies, no qualifying activity is required. Some life circumstances make a month count automatically. States may also offer short-term hardship exceptions.</p>
    {{TABLE:work-requirement-exemptions.csv::Exemptions, deemed-compliant months, and hardship exceptions}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="verify-h">
    <h3 id="verify-h">Verification and consequences</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>When checked:</strong> at application and at renewal (federally, states may check more often; California preliminary guidance checks only at application and six-month renewal, and not merely because you report a change). Counties must first try to verify from existing data (ex parte) before requesting paperwork.</li>
        <li><strong>New applicants (California):</strong> compliance is verified for the month immediately before the application month (the look-back month). If you first become compliant while the application is pending, aid begins the first day of the following month and earlier months are denied (ACWDL 25-30, preliminary).</li>
        <li><strong>Enrolled members (California):</strong> at each six-month renewal the county looks for compliance (or exemption/hardship) in <em>any one month</em> since the last determination &mdash; qualifying months need not be consecutive.</li>
        <li><strong>If the county cannot verify compliance:</strong> notice of noncompliance, <strong>30 calendar days</strong> to respond with coverage continuing (deemed received 5 days after the notice date; self-attested non-receipt is good cause). Only after checking every other basis of eligibility and offering a fair hearing may the county deny or discontinue (no later than the end of the following month under AB 2161).</li>
        <li><strong>After termination:</strong> reapply at any time &mdash; no fixed lockout. California preliminary guidance allows former members to return <em>without a new application</em> within a 90-day cure period once compliance is shown. <strong>But:</strong> losing Medi-Cal for noncompliance does <em>not</em> unlock subsidized Covered California coverage &mdash; DHCS says financial help is unavailable and only full-cost plans remain (see WR-17/CC-13 in the tables above).</li>
        <li><strong>Six-month renewals:</strong> section 71107 of P.L. 119-21 doubles renewal frequency for expansion adults (California: from March 1, 2027).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="kbd-note">Source: <a href="https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicaid-community-engagement-requirement-certain-individuals-interim-final-rule-comment-period-cms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS fact sheet on CMS-2454-IFC</a>; <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MEDIL-I26-25.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS MEDIL I26-25</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ca-h">
    <h3 id="ca-h">California's implementation</h3>
    <p>California is implementing the requirements for the MAGI New Adult Group. DHCS has been texting and mailing members since February/ August 2026, and the state enacted AB 2161 to add procedural protections. New copayments arrive October 1, 2028 at the earliest.</p>
    {{TABLE:california-implementation.csv::California (DHCS) implementation status and timeline}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="keydates-h">
    <h3 id="keydates-h">Key dates at a glance</h3>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li><span class="when">July 4, 2025</span> P.L. 119-21 signed (sections 71107 and 71119).</li>
      <li><span class="when">December 8, 2025</span> CMS initial guidance to states (information bulletin).</li>
      <li><span class="when">June 1, 2026</span> CMS interim final rule issued (effective July 31, 2026).</li>
      <li><span class="when">August 2026</span> DHCS begins mailing member outreach letters; texts began February 2026.</li>
      <li><span class="when">October 1 &ndash; December 31, 2026</span> Counties include the outreach letter with all new and returning applications.</li>
      <li><span class="when">January 1, 2027</span> Work and community engagement rules take effect; new applicants must show a compliant month before applying; retroactive coverage shortens to one month for childless adults.</li>
      <li><span class="when">March 1, 2027</span> Six-month eligibility checks begin in California; existing members are assessed at their first six-month renewal (the DHCS member letter tells current members the rules &ldquo;will not go into effect until March 2027&rdquo;).</li>
      <li><span class="when">October 1, 2028</span> Copayments begin for some adults (capped at 5% of household income).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="kbd-note">Note: CMS frames the deadline as &ldquo;no later than January 1, 2027,&rdquo; CRS describes &ldquo;effective beginning December 31, 2026,&rdquo; and DHCS's member letter says March 2027 for existing members &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</p>
  </section>
